**Answer: No.**

It does not follow in general that $\int_X g$ exists. The issue is that the hypothesis "$X\setminus Y$ has measure zero" gives no control over the values (or the boundedness) of $g$ on $X\setminus Y$, and for Riemann integrability over a bounded set the function must be bounded on that set.

**Counterexample.**

Work in $\mathbb{R}$ (so $m=1$). Define
$$
X=\{0\}\cup\Bigl\{\tfrac1n:n=1,2,3,\dots\Bigr\},\qquad Y=\{0\}.
$$
Both $X$ and $Y$ are compact (they are closed and bounded, and $X$ contains its only limit point $0$). Moreover
$$
X\setminus Y=\Bigl\{\tfrac1n:n\ge 1\Bigr\}
$$
is countable, hence has (Lebesgue) measure zero.

Define $g:X\to\mathbb{R}$ by
$$
g(0)=0,\qquad g\!\left(\tfrac1n\right)=n\quad(n\ge 1).
$$

*$g$ is integrable on $Y$.* The set $Y=\{0\}$ is a single point. Extending $g|_Y$ by $0$ off $Y$ gives the identically zero function on any enclosing rectangle, which is Riemann integrable with integral $0$. So $g$ is integrable on $Y$.

*$g$ is not integrable on $X$.* The function $g$ is unbounded on $X$, since $g(1/n)=n\to\infty$. A Riemann-integrable function on a bounded set must be bounded (the upper and lower sums cannot both be finite for an unbounded function). Therefore $g$ is not Riemann integrable on $X$, and $\int_X g$ does not exist.

**Why the obvious "measure-zero modification" argument fails.** One might hope to argue that since $X\setminus Y$ has measure zero, changing the integrand there does not affect integrability. That argument is valid *when the function is already known to be bounded on $X$*: if $f$ and $h$ are bounded functions on a rectangle that differ only on a set of measure zero, then integrability of one implies integrability of the other (with equal integrals). But here we have no a priori bound on $g$ over $X\setminus Y$. The hypothesis only concerns the *size* of $X\setminus Y$, not the *values* of $g$ on it, and an unbounded function on a measure-zero set still destroys Riemann integrability on the whole set. Thus the implication
$$
\text{$g$ integrable on $Y$}\;\Longrightarrow\;\text{$g$ integrable on $X$}
$$
is **false** in general.

